<%= render layout: 'blogs/blog_post_layout', locals: { article: article } do %>
  <p class="lead">Your AI agent just deleted 10,000 records because a user said "clean this up." Here's how to prevent it.</p>

  <h2>The Promise and Risk of Agentic AI</h2>
  <p>Agents that automate tasks end-to-end are powerful—and dangerous if they have broad permissions, no approval step, and no audit trail. Misinterpretation, permission abuse, cascading failures, and no undo create real incidents.</p>

  <h2>Real Agent Failures</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Refund agent approved a $50K refund when the user meant $50.</li>
    <li>Calendar agent deleted all meetings after a vague "clear my calendar" request.</li>
    <li>Integration agent sent an email to 100K users instead of one test recipient.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Governance Framework</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Scoped permissions</strong>—read-only vs write; data-level access (e.g. this tenant only).</li>
    <li><strong>Human-in-the-loop</strong>—critical actions require approval (refunds, bulk deletes, external sends).</li>
    <li><strong>Dry-run mode</strong>—show what the agent would do; don't execute until confirmed.</li>
    <li><strong>Confidence thresholds</strong>—low confidence → ask human.</li>
    <li><strong>Rate limiting</strong>—e.g. max 10 actions per minute per user.</li>
    <li><strong>Audit logging</strong>—every action, every decision, who triggered it.</li>
    <li><strong>Rollback</strong>—undo last N actions or revert to checkpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>Testing</strong>—adversarial scenarios (ambiguous instructions, malicious inputs).</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Compliance: SOC 2, HIPAA, and GDPR all expect access control, audit trails, and least privilege. We'll cover architecture for a safe agentic system and a governance wrapper in code. <a href="https://calendly.com/ahmadkamran/new-meeting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Schedule an AI agent governance review</a>.</p>
<% end %>
